WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.522 --> 00:00:07.857
&gt;&gt;&gt; Tonight, an incredible

00:00:07.990 --> 00:00:08.925
rescue caught on tape.

00:00:09.092 --> 00:00:10.760
A story of a bizarre crime on

00:00:10.893 --> 00:00:11.394
top of it.

00:00:11.527 --> 00:00:12.762
You have to see this.

00:00:12.895 --> 00:00:13.863
Take a look at this.

00:00:13.996 --> 00:00:15.631
The U.S. Coast guard was on a

00:00:15.765 --> 00:00:17.700
training mission when this boat

00:00:17.834 --> 00:00:18.067
capsized.

00:00:18.434 --> 00:00:22.505
The crew received a call from

00:00:22.638 --> 00:00:23.272
the vessel.

00:00:23.406 --> 00:00:27.777
They determind rough waves made

00:00:28.444 --> 00:00:30.446
a boat rescue too difficult.

00:00:30.446 --> 00:00:31.647
The man was rescued.

00:00:31.781 --> 00:00:33.483
After images of the rescue

00:00:33.616 --> 00:00:35.351
became public, police received a

00:00:35.485 --> 00:00:36.786
call from a port security chief

00:00:36.919 --> 00:00:39.188
saying that the boat had been

00:00:39.322 --> 00:00:40.790
stolen earlier that day.

00:00:40.923 --> 00:00:43.159
Here to help us unpack this,

00:00:43.292 --> 00:00:46.462
lieutenant Tripp haze who joins

00:00:46.462 --> 00:00:46.896
me by phone.

00:00:47.029 --> 00:00:51.300
He was flying the helicopter.

00:00:51.434 --> 00:00:53.669
That video is just incredible.

00:00:53.803 --> 00:00:55.438
We have other images that were

00:00:55.571 --> 00:00:56.739
provided by the coast guard that

00:00:56.873 --> 00:01:00.943
we are showing with this on the

00:01:01.077 --> 00:01:02.879
vantage point of being on the

00:01:03.012 --> 00:01:03.179
water.

00:01:03.312 --> 00:01:04.480
It's remarkable footage.

00:01:04.547 --> 00:01:05.415
Tell us about this.

00:01:05.548 --> 00:01:06.482
This must have been a very

00:01:06.616 --> 00:01:07.383
difficult rescue.

00:01:07.517 --> 00:01:08.050
What can you tell us?

00:01:08.484 --> 00:01:10.486
&gt;&gt; Yes, sir, absolutely.

00:01:10.486 --> 00:01:13.823
What I can tell you is, a

00:01:13.956 --> 00:01:14.891
complete collaborative effort

00:01:15.024 --> 00:01:16.492
across the coast guard and all

00:01:16.592 --> 00:01:17.326
responding agencies.

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:19.061
Like you said, you have footage

00:01:19.195 --> 00:01:19.962
from the water.

00:01:20.096 --> 00:01:20.830
That's because the first on

00:01:20.963 --> 00:01:22.498
scene was the coast guard's

00:01:22.498 --> 00:01:24.834
national motor life boat school.

00:01:24.967 --> 00:01:27.270
This is a complete collaboration

00:01:27.403 --> 00:01:30.173
between us, which was the

00:01:30.506 --> 00:01:31.274
helicopter crew from the

00:01:31.407 --> 00:01:32.909
advanced helicopter rescue

00:01:33.042 --> 00:01:35.211
school, national motor life boat

00:01:35.344 --> 00:01:37.280
school, local fish and cape,

00:01:37.413 --> 00:01:40.850
local sector command center at

00:01:40.983 --> 00:01:42.718
Columbia river.

00:01:42.852 --> 00:01:44.520
Everybody played their part.

00:01:44.554 --> 00:01:46.622
The way everything unfolded is

00:01:46.756 --> 00:01:51.260
once the boat was able to give

00:01:51.394 --> 00:01:52.962
him a life vest, it was

00:01:53.095 --> 00:01:54.530
determined getting him off by

00:01:54.630 --> 00:01:55.231
helicopter was going to be the

00:01:55.364 --> 00:01:58.568
best option.

00:01:58.701 --> 00:02:01.704
None of us expected the boat to

00:02:01.838 --> 00:02:02.538
overturn the way in which it

00:02:02.538 --> 00:02:04.540
did.

00:02:04.540 --> 00:02:06.776
The training and standardization

00:02:06.909 --> 00:02:07.844
and everything from the school

00:02:07.977 --> 00:02:09.979
that we attend, the whole

00:02:10.112 --> 00:02:11.180
purpose of us being there was

00:02:11.314 --> 00:02:15.818
for the advanced helicopter

00:02:15.952 --> 00:02:17.720
school and training in those

00:02:17.854 --> 00:02:18.120
conditions.

00:02:18.554 --> 00:02:21.591
That prepared us to execute

00:02:21.724 --> 00:02:21.891
that.

00:02:22.024 --> 00:02:23.326
&gt;&gt; Lieutenant, we are watching

00:02:23.459 --> 00:02:23.626
this.

00:02:23.759 --> 00:02:24.994
There's the boat turning over

00:02:25.127 --> 00:02:27.663
again, which is just incredible.

00:02:27.797 --> 00:02:29.432
Correct me if I'm mistaken.

00:02:29.565 --> 00:02:31.200
There's a coast guard swimmer in

00:02:31.334 --> 00:02:34.570
the water swimming over to the

00:02:34.570 --> 00:02:37.473
boat right before it capsizes

00:02:37.607 --> 00:02:39.408
and turns over, and he is okay?

00:02:39.542 --> 00:02:40.943
That swimmer is okay?

00:02:41.077 --> 00:02:43.145
&gt;&gt; Yes, sir.

00:02:43.279 --> 00:02:43.613
That's correct.

00:02:43.746 --> 00:02:50.586
That's petty officer branch

00:02:50.586 --> 00:02:50.720
Walton.

00:02:50.853 --> 00:02:51.587
He was one of the students in

00:02:51.721 --> 00:02:52.822
the school this week.

00:02:52.955 --> 00:02:58.227
This was his first after being

00:02:58.594 --> 00:02:58.728
qualified.

00:02:58.728 --> 00:03:01.197
Once we got on scene and the

00:03:01.330 --> 00:03:01.898
determinations were made that

00:03:02.031 --> 00:03:04.066
the helicopter recovery would be

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:06.903
the best option, we as a crew,

00:03:07.036 --> 00:03:09.672
speaking with branch, came up

00:03:09.805 --> 00:03:10.706
with a plan.

00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:12.775
Our flight mechanic operating

00:03:12.909 --> 00:03:15.444
the hoist that night just put

00:03:15.578 --> 00:03:17.446
branch down in the water.

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:18.614
Branch made his approach.

00:03:18.614 --> 00:03:20.750
What you can see there is as

00:03:20.883 --> 00:03:22.251
that wave is starting to break,

00:03:22.618 --> 00:03:23.953
the individual on the back of

00:03:24.086 --> 00:03:25.588
the boat kind of points up

00:03:25.721 --> 00:03:27.723
indicating something big is

00:03:27.857 --> 00:03:28.057
coming.

00:03:28.190 --> 00:03:28.858
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:03:28.991 --> 00:03:32.628
&gt;&gt; That's when branch dunk

00:03:32.662 --> 00:03:32.828
dived.

00:03:32.962 --> 00:03:34.764
He popped right up and

00:03:34.897 --> 00:03:36.632
immediately got back into rescue

00:03:36.732 --> 00:03:37.333
mode and was able to get the guy

00:03:37.466 --> 00:03:37.600
out.

00:03:37.733 --> 00:03:39.769
&gt;&gt; Wow.

00:03:39.902 --> 00:03:41.704
What an amazing survival story

00:03:41.837 --> 00:03:44.173
just on that point.

00:03:44.307 --> 00:03:46.642
I'm looking at this video.

00:03:46.742 --> 00:03:48.311
Before that boat turns over, it

00:03:48.644 --> 00:03:50.780
looks like I'm looking at people

00:03:50.913 --> 00:03:53.049
on the back of the boat.

00:03:53.182 --> 00:03:54.183
Everybody on board the boat you

00:03:54.317 --> 00:03:57.820
were rescuing, they lived as

00:03:57.954 --> 00:03:58.120
well?

00:03:58.254 --> 00:03:59.488
That's an incredible stroke of

00:03:59.622 --> 00:04:00.089
luck if that's the case.

00:04:00.222 --> 00:04:02.158
&gt;&gt; Yes, sir.

00:04:02.291 --> 00:04:03.526
We confirmed there was one

00:04:03.659 --> 00:04:05.962
individual on that boat.

00:04:06.095 --> 00:04:07.563
That's the one male in all black

00:04:07.697 --> 00:04:10.032
that you likely see there at the

00:04:10.166 --> 00:04:10.666
very stern.

00:04:10.666 --> 00:04:12.068
It does appear like when you

00:04:12.201 --> 00:04:12.902
look in the cabin there might be

00:04:13.035 --> 00:04:15.972
more people.

00:04:16.105 --> 00:04:18.708
Trust me, we had a guy in the

00:04:18.841 --> 00:04:20.743
back working a camera like

00:04:20.876 --> 00:04:21.043
crazy.

00:04:21.177 --> 00:04:22.044
We were able to confirm there

00:04:22.178 --> 00:04:23.879
was one person on board.

00:04:24.013 --> 00:04:26.349
We also confirmed from that

00:04:26.682 --> 00:04:27.083
individual when branch was able

00:04:27.216 --> 00:04:30.686
to pick him out of the water.

00:04:30.686 --> 00:04:31.520
&gt;&gt; This has to be the craziest

00:04:31.654 --> 00:04:33.889
rescue you have done.

00:04:34.023 --> 00:04:35.257
If you have done crazier,

00:04:35.391 --> 00:04:37.593
please, tell me more.

00:04:37.727 --> 00:04:40.296
This is just unbelievable.

00:04:40.696 --> 00:04:40.830
&gt;&gt; Yes, sir.

00:04:40.830 --> 00:04:43.132
It was unbelievable.

00:04:43.265 --> 00:04:45.668
I have to tell you, none of us

00:04:45.801 --> 00:04:47.803
expected the boat to overturn

00:04:47.937 --> 00:04:48.704
and that wave to break the way

00:04:48.704 --> 00:04:50.706
in which it did.

00:04:50.706 --> 00:04:52.041
But the training we are

00:04:52.174 --> 00:04:53.209
receiving, the standardization

00:04:53.342 --> 00:04:56.112
of mixing our air crews and none

00:04:56.245 --> 00:04:57.613
of us had flown with branch and

00:04:57.747 --> 00:04:59.749
we go out there and do it and do

00:04:59.882 --> 00:05:03.619
it safely just goes to really

00:05:03.753 --> 00:05:05.554
foot stomp and emphasize what

00:05:05.688 --> 00:05:06.355
we're doing and how we can do it

00:05:06.722 --> 00:05:09.358
safely.

00:05:09.492 --> 00:05:11.293
&gt;&gt; It underlines the incredible

00:05:11.427 --> 00:05:13.629
brave work that the coast guard

00:05:13.763 --> 00:05:14.730
does each and every day.

00:05:14.730 --> 00:05:17.299
You guys put your lives on the

00:05:17.433 --> 00:05:20.202
line keeping our coasts safe and

00:05:20.336 --> 00:05:21.570
getting involved in rescues like

00:05:21.704 --> 00:05:23.773
this where you put your lives on

00:05:23.906 --> 00:05:24.306
the line.

00:05:24.740 --> 00:05:26.842
A trainee in the water like you

00:05:26.976 --> 00:05:28.744
were describing, talk about -- I

00:05:28.744 --> 00:05:30.079
hope he passed with flying

00:05:30.212 --> 00:05:32.114
colors.

00:05:32.248 --> 00:05:32.915
This was just textbook stuff

00:05:33.049 --> 00:05:35.718
what you pulled off there.

00:05:35.851 --> 00:05:36.752
A team photo we have here at the

00:05:36.886 --> 00:05:38.888
end showing you together.

00:05:39.021 --> 00:05:39.588
Congratulations on a job well

00:05:39.722 --> 00:05:40.756
done.

00:05:40.756 --> 00:05:42.825
&gt;&gt; Yeah, thank you very much.

00:05:42.958 --> 00:05:44.126
Trust me, the first thing I did

00:05:44.260 --> 00:05:46.128
when I got out of the helicopter

00:05:46.262 --> 00:05:49.632
was find branch and give limb a

00:05:49.765 --> 00:05:51.367
-- him a hug.

00:05:51.500 --> 00:05:52.301
&gt;&gt; I bet you did.

00:05:52.435 --> 00:05:53.469
And called your loved ones.

00:05:53.602 --> 00:05:54.203
&gt;&gt; The second thing I did, yes,

00:05:54.336 --> 00:05:56.772
sir.

00:05:56.806 --> 00:05:58.040
&gt;&gt; Thanks very much for your

00:05:58.174 --> 00:05:58.374
time.

00:05:58.774 --> 00:05:59.875
Hats off to everybody else.

00:06:00.009 --> 00:06:00.776
Give everybody a pat on the back

00:06:00.776 --> 00:06:00.943
for us.

